SQL     : Fixed schema, ACID, strong consistency
          Great for: transactions, complex queries
          Examples: MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle

ACID    : Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability
          Critical for financial / transactional data

NoSQL   : Flexible schema, horizontal scale
  Types : Document   → MongoDB (user profiles)
          Key-Value  → Redis (cache, sessions)
          Wide-Column→ Cassandra (messaging, time-series)
          Graph      → Neo4j (social networks)

Real use: Most systems use BOTH
          SQL  → financial, transactional data
          NoSQL→ scale, flexibility, speed

Memory  : SQL = spreadsheet (rigid, powerful)
          NoSQL = folder of files (flexible, fast)

Key rule: NEVER say one is "better" — ask what
          the use case is first
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
```

